# SyncroMove: Low Level Design

**Project 1: Sequence Prediction (Medium). Predicting a user's next location.**

SyncroMove is a web app that predicts the three places a logged-in user is most likely to visit
next, from their own previous check-ins. The prediction is a **fixed, explainable algorithm**
(Markov transitions + time of day + frequency + recency). There is no model training, no weight
fitting and no neural network.

Headline result (from `docs/evaluation_results.json`): **{{pct:methods.full.top3}} top-3 accuracy** on
{{int:dataset.test_checkins}} unseen New York check-ins, against {{pct:methods.most_visited.top3}} for the "most visited places" baseline.

---

## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 Problem
Given a user's past check-ins (place + time), predict where they go next. Show the top 3 places,
each with a likelihood and a one-line reason.

### 1.2 Approach
A scoring rule runs on the backend at request time. It reads the user's own history from the
database, scores every candidate place with four simple signals (plus a tiny city-popularity
tie-breaker), and returns the three highest. A new check-in changes the next prediction
immediately, because there is nothing to retrain.

### 1.3 Users
| Actor | Can do |
|---|---|
| Visitor | Register, log in |
| Logged-in user | Record check-ins, see top-3 predictions with reasons and signal breakdown, pick the prediction time, see accuracy, export or delete their data |
| Host admin | Runs `scripts/import_dataset.py` and `scripts/evaluate.py` on the machine. There is no admin UI |

### 1.4 Scope
In scope: login/registration, check-in history, top-3 next-place prediction with reasons,
accuracy card, data export and deletion.

Out of scope: model training, live GPS tracking, tile-server maps, mobile app, social features.

### 1.5 Important constraint
The dataset is **New York City, April 2012 – February 2013**, and venues have **no names**, only
a category, such as "Coffee Shop", and coordinates. A new user elsewhere cannot produce
meaningful NYC check-ins. Registration therefore offers **"Load a sample history"**: a
**made-up year of check-ins at real New York venues**, copied into the new account so predictions
work at once. The user can then add check-ins by searching places by category; results are listed
nearest to their last check-in. The UI says the history is made up, on the sign-up form and under the map.

**Sample histories** (`scripts/generate_synthetic.py`, seed 42). There are 25 imaginary people,
each with a home, a work or study place and regular places (coffee, gym, groceries, lunch, dinner,
bar, park, a friend's home, shops), all real dataset venues chosen near home or work. A weekly
routine with random variation produces 358 days of check-ins: work-day commutes, gym days, Friday
bars, weekend parks and brunch, about 25% of weekend afternoons at somewhere new nearby, a 5–11
day holiday with no check-ins, and only 18–32% of visits checked in. The result is 305–600
check-ins and 24–40 different places per person (the brief asked for at least 10). On sign-up, the
copy is moved forward by whole weeks so it ends in the past 7 days. Whole weeks keep every visit's
weekday and local time, so the history always covers the past year. The real 2012–13 samples
(`sample_histories.json.gz`, 25 dataset users with at least 10 places over at least 9 months) can
be used instead via `SAMPLES_PATH`.

**Accuracy on made-up data is not the real result.** Generated routines are far more regular than
real people: the full rule scores about 74% top-3 on these histories against {{pct:methods.full.top3}} on
the real data. The accuracy card and §5 report only the real-data evaluation.

